  "blocks": {
    "question-body": {
      "id": "question-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "question-ask",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A circle is the smoothest shape there is, without a single corner. Yet chain a few turning circles end to end, and a pen on the last one can draw a musical note with sharp corners. This lesson answers: how many circles, how large, and how fast?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "series-equation",
          "type": "math",
          "value": "z(t)=c_0+\\sum_{n\\ne 0} c_n e^{\\,int},\\qquad 0\\le t<2\\pi"
        },
        {
          "id": "question-reading",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Read each point of the plane as a complex number z = x + iy. Each term cₙeⁱⁿᵗ is a circle: its radius is |cₙ|, it turns n times while the pen goes round once, and the sign of n sets the direction."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "circles-body": {
      "id": "circles-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "circles-explain",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "One circle can only draw a circle. Put a smaller circle on its rim, turning twice the other way, and the pen's curve is bent into three corners."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "two-circles-equation",
          "type": "math",
          "value": "z(t)=e^{\\,it}+0.36\\,e^{-2it}"
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "circles-scene": {
      "id": "circles-scene",
      "type": "component",
      "definitionId": "circle-chain",
      "parameters": {}
    },
    "fit-body": {
      "id": "fit-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "fit-explain",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "First take 1024 evenly spaced points along the outline of the note, then compute each circle's radius and starting angle with the discrete Fourier transform. The figure keeps only the N largest circles, chained by size. The dashed line is the target outline; on the right is the error: the root mean square distance between each point and the pen, relative to the size of the outline."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "dft-equation",
          "type": "math",
          "value": "c_n=\\frac1M\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1} z_k\\,e^{-2\\pi i nk/M},\\qquad M=1024"
        },
        {
          "id": "fit-corners",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Watch the corners: with few circles the curve overshoots each corner and swings back, leaving small ripples. With more circles the ripples hug the corner more closely, but they never vanish completely."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "fit-scene": {
      "id": "fit-scene",
      "type": "component",
      "definitionId": "fourier-fit",
